---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI manual builds" activeNav="/guides/cli/builds/manual-builds">
  <h1>Manual builds</h1>
  <p>
    A manual build is one where your CI does the work and reports back. The platform stores
    the record, the log, and the artifacts; it does not start anything. This is the default
    reporting mode, and the only mode that accepts status and log writes from the CLI.
  </p>

  <h2>The lifecycle</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create the build and keep its id.</li>
    <li>Stream each step's output into the build log.</li>
    <li>Upload artifacts.</li>
    <li>Report the terminal status — or let <code>stream --manage-status</code> do it.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Create and capture the id</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--request-uuid</code> makes creation idempotent. Pass your own value derived from
    something stable in the job, and a retried CI step reuses the same build instead of
    creating a duplicate:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json builds create \\
    --profile production \\
    --request-uuid "$CI_JOB_ID" \\
    --context '{"runner":"github-actions","commit":"'"$GITHUB_SHA"'"}' \\
    --tag android:#3ddc84 |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)
export BUILD_ID`} />
  <p>
    <code>jq -er</code> matters: <code>-e</code> makes the pipeline fail when the id is
    missing, instead of exporting an empty variable that every later command silently
    misuses.
  </p>

  <h2>Stream step output</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stream</code> runs a command, mirrors its combined output to your terminal, and
    uploads it to the build log while it runs. Each step gets its own separators in the log:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Install -- pnpm install
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Build   -- pnpm build
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Test    -- pnpm test`} />
  <p>
    The CLI exits with the wrapped command's status, so a failing step fails the CI job
    normally. If the command succeeds but the log upload fails, it exits <code>1</code> —
    a build whose log silently vanished is not a success.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no <code>eval</code>. Shell syntax needs an explicit shell:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Package \\
  -- sh -c 'cd android && ./gradlew bundleRelease | tee build.log'`} />

  <h3>Managing status automatically</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--manage-status</code> sets <code>running</code> before the command and
    <code>completed</code> or <code>failed</code> after it, based on the exit status. Put it
    on the first step only — otherwise each step flips the build back to
    <code>running</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --manage-status --step Install -- pnpm install
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --step Build -- pnpm build`} />

  <h3>Aborting on cancellation</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--track</code> polls the build while the command runs and aborts it if someone
    cancels the build in the UI, so a cancelled build stops burning runner minutes:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --track --step Build -- pnpm build`} />

  <h3>Chunk size</h3>
  <p>
    Output is uploaded in chunks of 64 KiB by default, and the API rejects anything above
    256 KiB. Lower it for chatty builds where you want the log to appear sooner:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --chunk-bytes 16384 --step Build -- pnpm build`} />

  <h2>Reporting status by hand</h2>
  <p>
    Without <code>--manage-status</code>, report transitions yourself. Valid statuses are
    <code>running</code>, <code>completed</code>, <code>failed</code>, and
    <code>cancelled</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds patch "$BUILD_ID" --status running
# ... run the build ...
appmctl builds patch "$BUILD_ID" --status completed`} />
  <p>
    A transition into a terminal state is final. Patching a build that is already
    <code>completed</code> answers <code>409</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Appending to the log directly</h2>
  <p>
    <code>logs post</code> appends a single chunk, which is enough for short notes that are
    not command output:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl logs post --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --content "Signing with the 2027 keystore."`} />

  <h2>A complete job</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`set -euo pipefail

BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json builds create --profile production \\
    --request-uuid "$CI_JOB_ID" \\
    --context '{"runner":"gitlab","commit":"'"$CI_COMMIT_SHA"'"}' |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)
export BUILD_ID

# Any failure from here must still land as a failed build, not a build stuck
# in "running" forever.
trap 'appmctl builds patch "$BUILD_ID" --status failed || true' ERR

appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --manage-status --track --step Install -- pnpm install
appmctl stream --build-id "$BUILD_ID" --track --step Build -- pnpm build

appmctl artifacts upload --build-id "$BUILD_ID" \\
  --name app-release.aab --file build/app-release.aab
appmctl files upload --file build/mapping.txt \\
  --metadata '{"build_id":"'"$BUILD_ID"'"}'

appmctl builds patch "$BUILD_ID" --status completed --tag 1.4.0:#f59e0b`} />
  <p>
    The <code>trap</code> is the part people leave out. Without it, a runner that dies between
    the first and last step leaves a build that claims to be running forever.
  </p>

  <h2>Why manual mode is required</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stream</code>, <code>logs post</code>, and status transitions all reject builds that
    a provider reports, with <code>409</code>. Those records mirror an external system, and
    letting two writers disagree about the same build would make the record untrustworthy. See
    <a href="/guides/cli/builds/automated-builds">Automated builds</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>builds:write</code> to create and patch, <code>logs:write</code> to stream or post,
    <code>artifacts:write</code> to upload. A reporting-only key needs nothing else.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
